<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jeremiah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 650–570 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jeremiah was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> prophet in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> from the late 7th century BCE until after its destruction by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> in 587 BCE. He warned that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> would fall for its unfaithfulness and urged submission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, which led to his imprisonment under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zedekiah">Zedekiah</a>. After the fall of Jerusalem he was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-jeremiah">Book of Jeremiah</a> is attributed to him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jeremiah.jpg" alt="Jeremiah" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Horace Vernet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SA_160-Jeremia_op_de_puinhopen_van_Jeruzalem.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Prophets such as Isaiah, Micah and <b>Jeremiah</b> criticised social injustice and religious practice.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-judah">Kingdom of Judah · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…three parts: the Torah, the five books of Moses; the Nevi’im, the Prophets, which include the histories of Israel from Joshua to the kings and the books of prophets such as Isaiah, <b>Jeremiah</b> and Ezekiel; and the Ketuvim, the Writings, among them the Psalms, Proverbs, Job and Daniel.</blockquote><a href="/religion/judaism">Judaism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>…texts of the 7th century BCE call the same people Ishkuzai, a name usually identified with the Ashkenaz whom the prophet <b>Jeremiah</b> lists beside Ararat and Minni, and the Persians called the nomads of the steppe Saka; Herodotus remarks that the Persians gave that name to all Scythians.</blockquote><a href="/culture/scythians">Scythians · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Scholars compare it with the biblical Book of Lamentations on the fall of Jerusalem in the sixth century BC, which is similar in style and theme, and with laments in <b>Jeremiah</b>, Ezekiel and Psalm 137; Philip S. Alexander has set some of its lines beside Lamentations 2:17.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lament-for-ur">Lament for Ur</a></li><li><blockquote>Rabbinical sources attributed it to the prophet <b>Jeremiah</b>, and the Septuagint heads it &quot;For David.</blockquote><a href="/songs/psalm-137">Psalm 137</a></li><li><blockquote>…and in The Wild Bunch (1969), as an ironic counterpoint to a massacre on screen, and it is heard in later Westerns such as Hang 'Em High (1968) and <b>Jeremiah</b> Johnson (1972), as well as in Hobson's Choice (1954), Elmer Gantry (1960) and The Trip to Bountiful (1985).</blockquote><a href="/songs/shall-we-gather-at-the-river">Shall We Gather at the River?</a></li></ul></section>
